    13	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    14	# -------------------------------------
    15	# a) Concrete behavior
    16	#   - Source task: each source color owns its own local legend and its own teal
    17	#     stencil; stamping is color-matched and monochrome.
    18	#   - This combo collapses the "many rules" axis and keeps only one legend pair
    19	#     and one global template. The visible template is now multicolor:
    20	#       * teal cells are the mutable backbone
    21	#       * non-teal accent cells are fixed paint that must be copied unchanged
    22	#   - Example trace:
    23	#       input may show one red->yellow legend, one large teal cluster with grey
    24	#       and blue diagonal accents, and scattered isolated source pixels in red,
    25	#       green, and orange. In the output every scattered source pixel produces the
    26	#       same motif: the teal backbone turns yellow while the grey/blue accents stay
    27	#       grey/blue. The original legend and global template disappear.
    28	#   - Difference from the source task:
    29	#       * source color no longer selects a rule
    30	#       * there is only one template in the whole grid
    31	#       * copied motifs are not monochrome
    32	#
    33	# b) Complexity calibration
    34	#   - The matching logic is simpler, so the template itself is made richer:
    35	#     4-10 teal cells plus 2-4 accent cells using 2-3 accent colors.
    36	#   - Every generated instance uses at least two scattered source colors. This
    37	#     makes the D9 change observable instead of silently degenerating into
    38	#     "one source color repeated".
    39	#   - There are no bystanders. Under D9 every template-external colored singleton
    40	#     should behave as a source, so preserved bystanders would contradict the rule.
    41	#
    42	# c) Implicit assumption audit
    43	#   - Original main() assumed "source color identifies the rule". That is removed:
    44	#     solver now finds the unique legend pair near the lone teal component and then
    45	#     treats every other non-template colored singleton as a source center.
    46	#   - Original discovery stored only teal coordinates. That breaks under D6 because
    47	#     accent cells would be lost. Solver now extracts every non-background cell
    48	#     within Chebyshev 1 of the teal component as part of the template.
    49	#   - Original preservation logic copied unrelated colored pixels through. That is
    50	#     wrong here because template-external colored pixels are precisely the source
    51	#     markers that must be consumed.
    52	#   - New solver assumption: there is exactly one teal connected component and
    53	#     exactly one colored 4-neighbor pair whose continuation reaches it. Generator
